            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Overall, we need to understand why it is the webservers job to verify a hostname.  the backend should be sending appropriate error messages on bad input."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "priyair",
                        "email": "priya_irudayaraj@yahoo.co.in",
                        "username": "priyair"
                    },
                    "message": "This exact validation pattern exists in:\n\nredfish-core/lib/ethernet.hpp - validates HostName property\nredfish-core/lib/hypervisor.hpp - validates HostName property\nMy change simply extends this pattern to NTP server validation for consistency.\n\nbmcweb server ensures the following:\n1. The Redfish specification requires specific error message formats (PropertyValueFormatError). If the backend returns a generic error, how do we translate that to the required Redfish-compliant format?\n\n2. Prevents potentially expensive backend operations (DNS lookups, D-Bus calls, systemd service restarts) for obviously invalid input."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> My change simply extends this pattern to NTP server validation for consistency.\n\nYes, there are other places that violate this.  That's not a reason to continue to make it worse.  Ideally these would be rolled into the backend.\n\n> how do we translate that to the required Redfish-compliant format?\n\nThere's lots of prior art there.  Take a look at AccountService which does a good job of this error conversion.\n\n> Prevents potentially expensive backend operations (DNS lookups, D-Bus calls, systemd service restarts) for obviously invalid input.\n\nI'm not understanding this.  The backend can choose to not do this if it wants."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "priyair",
                        "email": "priya_irudayaraj@yahoo.co.in",
                        "username": "priyair"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi @ed@tanous.net Added validation is phosphor-networkd as below, bur for curl request \nPATCH -d '{\"NTP\":{\"NTPServers\":[\"-8.-8.-8.-8\"], \"ProtocolEnabled\":true}}\n\nMultiple setDbusProperty calls are made (one per ethernet interface) from bmcweb\nEach call is asynchronous and completes at different times\n1) First call (invalid NTP): Sets error to 400 \n2) Second call (ProtocolEnabled): Succeeds and sets status to 204\nOVERWRITES the 400! hence does not raise the propertyValueFormat error. \nTo prevent errors from being overwritten by subsequent successful operations we need to address the issue in bmcweb.\n \nhttps://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-networkd/+/88215"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> Hi @ed@tanous.net Added validation is phosphor-networkd as below, bur for curl request \n> PATCH -d '{\"NTP\":{\"NTPServers\":[\"-8.-8.-8.-8\"], \"ProtocolEnabled\":true}}\n> \n> Multiple setDbusProperty calls are made (one per ethernet interface) from bmcweb\n> Each call is asynchronous and completes at different times\n> 1) First call (invalid NTP): Sets error to 400 \n> 2) Second call (ProtocolEnabled): Succeeds and sets status to 204\n> OVERWRITES the 400! hence does not raise the propertyValueFormat error. \n\nYep, this is a bug.  We should fix this.  Look at how aggregation does overwriting of the error code here.\n\nhttps://github.com/openbmc/bmcweb/blob/f99bc45d579b751e693bd1655dfd623bd84475f8/redfish-core/include/utils/error_code.hpp#L38\n\n> To prevent errors from being overwritten by subsequent successful operations we need to address the issue in bmcweb.\n>  \n> https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-networkd/+/88215\n\nAgreed. Keep in mind, even with this patch the backend could still reject the request."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/include/utils/network_utils.hpp",
                    "line": 39,
                    "reviewer": {
